boy to explain what a production  designer does I think of myself as the  hand of the director I read a script  absorb all that information but my real  job is to understand how the director  reads the script and what he sees and  what he wants to visually accomplish and  you almost have to become I don't know  do you have to be a mind reader or you  just have to Intuit the director and  personality the director and where he  wants to go in pre-production you know  we're given the script and the budget  and we try to do the best we can to get  this the physical things than somebody's  home who's a character or any of the  sets whatever they would be from a  palace to a garbage dump I mean it's all  is everything gardens hotels all of it  and we there do a practical location and  change things according to our script or  we build it from scratch so we have it  just the way we want it the primary  responsibility is responding to the  script obviously then trying to make  your vision fit within the restraints of  any budget and thirdly I suppose is  maintaining a good working relationship  with the people around you which i think  is it's kind of quite high on the  priority list it's a lot of work and I  don't work alone I work with the group  and in the state so we have what we call  a lead man who runs my whole crew and  then we have set dressers who move all  the things around and I work with a  buyer and on a bigger project I have two  buyers and a coordinator it's it's a  very fast-paced operation it's never  boring though yeah I mean I think if  you're trying to create a world you  always need to be able to do it as  accurately as you possibly can and  that's when you rely heavily on a team  of people that around you that work in  my department without whom you know you  are only as good as the people that in  the team that you're working  I can say that with my hand on my heart  so they are really important part of the  whole process so you have a you cannot  quite often you know talk to them spend  hours researching that's the main part  of this job is getting good reference  it's the timing it's it's very it's I  guess you could call it interior  decorating  speed it up it's very fast pace we  usually pay attention to the first week  of shooting because things always change  they've changed schedules according to  actors availability or they've changed  the script so as soon as it's certain  what the first week of shooting will be  for a film we we want to be completely  100% ready for that at the end of  pre-production which is usually eight  weeks eight to ten weeks for an average  film you know from the time I was a  child I was somehow interested in the  theater and you know whether it was  acting or building sets and I can  remember you know as a kid a book a  Samuel French edition of wait until dark  and there was a diagram of the stage  that of the ground side of the stage  said back a book and I built a model of  that in a shoebox well I started off  doing a theater design degree and I  spent ten years doing theater where you  do everything I mean everything build  the set sometimes on low-budget stuff  you designed the costumes and then I  reached a point where I was sort of I  was in a transitional period with that  and a friend of mine said look do you  want to come and help me on these short  films so and we did everything out the  back of a van and we didn't go to high  shops we just skip hunted for props  eglee will tell you this one of the  reasons he was interested in my  designing Life of Pi was because of my  theater background and he had a sense  that shooting a film 3d to him felt  somewhat like  feeder the sort of the dimension of  theater and and the way perspective  works in theater so he kind of liked the  idea that that was my background that  that was where I came from well I think  multitasking is pretty important you  have to be able to have a lot going on  at once and not get rattled about it and  you know a lot of social skills and  getting along with anybody anywhere you  know you could be in a millionaire's  home you know and they come and visit  when you've changed it all and you have  to be able to appease them if they don't  like the way it looks and you know but  usually the location department works  with you about situations like that and  you just have to be able to fit into any  situation anywhere and try and be as  comfortable as possible about it and  it's you know that can be challenging  sometimes and sometimes it's a wonderful  surprise and you meet people that you  know for the rest of your life I think  my advice to anybody would be if you  really want to pursue it then don't stop  get as much experience as you can in any  field of any part of the filmmaking  business first of all you just have to  have it in your heart that just has to  be the way you see things that you read  a story or hear a story and you think of  it in visual terms I think the training  would be in art history and interior  design and full-on you know production  courses that would be available and  internships with a set decorator if  that's possible so you can shadow  somebody to really see what it's like  and if it would be for you you know I  think if you believe in something if you  if you love doing something enough  you'll get there anyway it's just a  matter of how you do it assist you know  getting assisting people getting your  way in and in any particular way phoning  up people like me you know knocking on  doors so if you have that in your heart  I think the things that you have to sort  of bear in mind is that you have to  be respectful of the stories that you  read and listen to your director so I  think if you could do that maybe control  a budget and you have it in your heart  to design that you'll have a successful  career
 

